            <div class=3D"rendered-content"><h2>1) The Use-Case, Reconstruc=
ted</h2><p>The use case under evaluation is a data-driven, algorithmic appr=
oach to the allocation of scarce homeless services, as presented in the art=
icle =E2=80=9CFair and Efficient Allocation of Scarce Resources Based on Pr=
edicted Outcomes: Implications for Homeless Service Delivery=E2=80=9D by Ku=
be, Das, and Fowler (2023). The decision problem addressed is the efficient=
 and fair distribution of limited housing and support services to homeless =
households within a metropolitan area in the United States, with the overar=
ching goal of minimizing the rate of reentry into the homeless system. The =
use case leverages machine learning, specifically Bayesian Additive Regress=
ion Trees (BART), to generate counterfactual predictions of individual hous=
eholds=E2=80=99 probabilities of reentry into homelessness under different =
service assignments. These predictions feed into an integer programming opt=
imization that aims to allocate households to services so as to minimize ex=
pected system-wide reentries, subject to capacity constraints on each servi=
ce type.</p><p>The decision context is one of chronic scarcity and complexi=
ty: homeless service providers operate under limited resources, fluctuating=
 service availability, and imperfect information about household needs and =
service efficacy. The stakeholders include homeless individuals and familie=
s seeking assistance, service providers, policymakers, and the broader comm=
unity affected by homelessness and its social costs. The target outcome is =
a reduction in the number of households reentering homelessness within two =
years of service exit, operationalized as a proxy for successful interventi=
on and system efficiency.</p><p>The moral rationale explicitly foregrounded=
 in the use case is twofold: first, to improve the efficiency of homeless s=
ervice delivery by better matching services to individual needs, thereby re=
ducing waste and unmet demand; second, to address fairness and equity conce=
rns inherent in algorithmic allocation, acknowledging that some households =
may be disadvantaged by reallocation and that systematic biases in data and=
 service provision may perpetuate inequities. The authors emphasize the imp=
ortance of transparency, accountability, and human interpretability in algo=
rithmic decision-making, and propose constraints on the optimization to lim=
it increases in individual reentry risk, thus attempting to balance efficie=
ncy gains with fairness considerations.</p><p>Implicit value claims include=
 the prioritization of aggregate system-level outcomes (minimizing total re=
entries) as a moral good, the assumption that predictive accuracy and count=
erfactual modeling can ethically guide resource allocation, and the accepta=
nce of trade-offs whereby some individuals may experience worse outcomes fo=
r the sake of overall system improvement. The use case assumes that reentry=
 probability is a valid and ethically relevant metric of service success, t=
hat administrative data accurately capture relevant household characteristi=
cs and outcomes, and that capacity constraints and service types are approp=
riately modeled.</p><p>Success in this use case is defined primarily in ter=
ms of predicted reductions in reentry rates, with secondary attention to di=
stributional effects and fairness constraints. The use case assumes that al=
gorithmic optimization can yield Pareto improvements or at least acceptable=
 trade-offs, and that human oversight (e.g., service worker overrides) can =
mitigate ethical risks. The approach privileges consequentialist metrics of=
 aggregate welfare (reentry minimization) while attempting to incorporate f=
airness constraints that limit harm to individuals, reflecting a hybrid con=
cern for both outcomes and procedural justice.</p><h2>2) The Framework, Rec=
onstructed</h2><p>The ethical framework for evaluation is deontology, a nor=
mative moral theory grounded in the primacy of duties, rights, and categori=
cal norms over consequentialist considerations. Deontology derives from the=
 Greek =E2=80=9Cdeon=E2=80=9D (duty) and =E2=80=9Clogos=E2=80=9D (study or =
science), and is centrally concerned with the moral assessment of choices b=
ased on their conformity to moral norms rather than solely on their outcome=
s. The framework distinguishes itself sharply from consequentialism by hold=
ing that certain actions are categorically forbidden or required regardless=
 of their consequences, and that moral agents have agent-relative reasons a=
nd permissions that protect the integrity of their agency and respect for p=
ersons.</p><p>Core principles of deontology include the priority of the Rig=
ht over the Good, meaning that moral norms (duties, prohibitions, permissio=
ns) are not to be overridden by considerations of maximizing good consequen=
ces. Deontological theories typically emphasize categorical obligations=E2=
=80=94norms that hold universally and unconditionally=E2=80=94and categoric=
al permissions, which allow actions even if they do not maximize good outco=
mes. The framework recognizes agent-relative reasons, which are reasons tha=
t apply specifically to the moral agent in question, allowing for partialit=
y to one=E2=80=99s own projects, relationships, and agency.</p><p>Deontolog=
y is often taxonomized into agent-centered and patient-centered theories. A=
gent-centered deontology focuses on the moral agent=E2=80=99s intentions, a=
ctions, and agency, emphasizing duties that protect the agent=E2=80=99s mor=
al integrity and prohibit intending or causing wrongful acts (e.g., killing=
 innocents). Patient-centered deontology centers on the rights of individua=
ls as patients or recipients of actions, often emphasizing the prohibition =
against using others as mere means without consent, and grounding duties in=
 respect for persons=E2=80=99 rights.</p><p>The framework includes doctrine=
s such as the Doctrine of Double Effect and the Doctrine of Doing and Allow=
ing, which distinguish between intending and foreseeing harm, causing and a=
llowing harm, and other fine-grained distinctions that delimit the scope of=
 categorical duties. Deontology also grapples with the paradox of deontolog=
y (how to handle situations where violating a duty prevents more violations=
), conflicts of duties, and moral catastrophes (dire consequences from stri=
ct adherence to duties).</p><p>Deontology=E2=80=99s scope conditions includ=
e the requirement that moral agents respect categorical norms even when doi=
ng so may not maximize overall welfare, and that moral reasons are not redu=
cible to aggregative calculations of good states of affairs. The framework =
demands that moral agents not justify wrongful acts by appealing to better =
consequences, and that they maintain the separateness of persons by respect=
ing individual rights and agency.</p><p>Typical failure modes of deontology=
 include excessive rigidity leading to moral fanaticism, difficulty resolvi=
ng conflicts of duties, and challenges in handling uncertainty about outcom=
es. Critics argue that some deontological distinctions (e.g., intending vs.=
 foreseeing) may be conceptually unclear or morally insignificant. The fram=
ework is also sensitive to metaethical commitments, with some versions more=
 hospitable to nonnatural realism, conventionalism, or Divine command theor=
y, and less so to naturalist realism.</p><p>The decision procedure in deont=
ology involves assessing whether an action conforms to categorical norms (d=
uties, prohibitions, permissions), considering agent-relative reasons and t=
he nature of agency involved (intention, causation), and respecting the rig=
hts of patients. Consequences may be relevant only insofar as they do not j=
ustify violating categorical norms. When conflicts arise, deontology may ap=
peal to specificationism (fine-grained norms with exceptions), prima facie =
duties, or threshold deontology (allowing exceptions beyond a high threshol=
d of consequences), though these responses have their own challenges.</p><h=
2>3) Principle-by-Principle Deep Critical Evaluation</h2><p>The use case=E2=
=80=99s ethical evaluation through the lens of deontology requires a carefu=
l, principle-by-principle analysis of its alignment and tensions with deont=
ological commitments. Central to this evaluation is the framework=E2=80=99s=
 insistence on the priority of categorical duties and the protection of ind=
ividual rights and agency over mere consequentialist aggregation.</p><p>Fir=
st, the use case aligns with deontology in its explicit recognition of fair=
ness and equity concerns, and its attempt to incorporate constraints that l=
imit harm to individuals by preventing large increases in their predicted p=
robability of reentry. This reflects a deontological sensitivity to the rig=
hts and interests of individual households, acknowledging that some may be =
worse off under an optimized allocation and that such harm must be constrai=
ned. The use case=E2=80=99s proposal to allow human overrides of algorithmi=
c decisions further respects the agent-relative prerogatives of service wor=
kers and the moral complexity of individual cases, aligning with deontology=
=E2=80=99s emphasis on the moral agency of persons and the importance of in=
tentions and deliberations beyond mere algorithmic outputs.</p><p>Moreover,=
 the use case=E2=80=99s attention to transparency, accountability, and inte=
rpretability resonates with deontological demands for respect and justifica=
tion in moral decision-making. By foregrounding these concerns, the use cas=
e implicitly acknowledges that moral agents (service providers) must be abl=
e to understand and justify their actions in accordance with moral norms, n=
ot merely follow opaque consequentialist calculations.</p><p>However, signi=
ficant tensions arise from the use case=E2=80=99s core reliance on conseque=
ntialist metrics and optimization. The primary objective is to minimize agg=
regate reentry rates, a quintessentially consequentialist goal that evaluat=
es actions solely by their outcomes. This conflicts with the deontological =
principle that some actions or allocations may be categorically forbidden o=
r required regardless of their aggregate consequences. For example, the use=
 case accepts that 21% of households are allocated to services that increas=
e their probability of reentry, a trade-off justified by overall system imp=
rovement. From a deontological standpoint, this raises the question of whet=
her it is morally permissible to impose harm on some individuals for the sa=
ke of aggregate benefits, especially when such harm involves violating thei=
r rights or agency.</p><p>Deontology demands that individual rights and dut=
ies not be overridden by aggregate welfare calculations. The use case=E2=80=
=99s acceptance of harm to some households for the benefit of others risks =
violating this principle, unless it can be shown that no categorical duty i=
s breached or that the harms do not constitute wrongful acts. The use case =
does not explicitly address whether reallocations that increase reentry ris=
k for some households violate categorical duties or rights, nor does it pro=
vide a principled justification grounded in deontological norms for permitt=
ing such harm. This omission represents a critical under-specification from=
 the deontological perspective.</p><p>The use case=E2=80=99s fairness const=
raint limiting increases in reentry probability to 5 percentage points is a=
 pragmatic attempt to mitigate harm, but it remains a consequentialist bala=
ncing act rather than a categorical norm. Deontology would require a more p=
rincipled prohibition against harming individuals beyond their consent or w=
ithout just cause, not merely a threshold of acceptable harm. The use case=
=E2=80=99s approach resembles threshold deontology, but without a clear art=
iculation of the normative status of the threshold or the categorical dutie=
s involved. This lack of normative grounding weakens alignment with deontol=
ogy.</p><p>Another key deontological principle is the respect for agency, p=
articularly the prohibition against intending harm and the importance of in=
tentions in constituting moral responsibility. The use case=E2=80=99s algor=
ithmic allocation is based on predicted probabilities and statistical corre=
lations, not on the intentions or mental states of agents. This raises the =
question of whether the harms imposed by allocation decisions can be unders=
tood as intended or merely foreseen risks, and whether the moral agency of =
decision-makers is properly engaged. Deontology requires that agents not in=
tend wrongful harm even if they foresee it; it is unclear how algorithmic d=
ecisions, mediated by human actors, respect this distinction. The use case =
does not elaborate on how intentions are formed or assessed in the allocati=
on process, leaving a gap in alignment with agent-centered deontology.</p><=
p>The use case=E2=80=99s reliance on administrative data and predictive mod=
eling also raises concerns about consent and the use of persons as means. P=
atient-centered deontology prohibits using others merely as means without t=
heir consent. The algorithmic allocation uses data about households to assi=
gn them to services that may increase or decrease their risk of reentry, po=
tentially treating them as means to optimize system outcomes. The use case =
does not discuss whether households consent to such algorithmic use of thei=
r data or to the reallocation decisions themselves. This absence of explici=
t consent or participatory justification is a significant divergence from d=
eontological requirements.</p><p>Furthermore, the use case implicitly aggre=
gates harms and benefits across individuals, treating reentry probabilities=
 as commensurable and summable states of affairs. Deontology, especially in=
 its patient-centered forms, resists such aggregation on the grounds of the=
 separateness of persons and the non-comparability of individual rights vio=
lations. The use case=E2=80=99s optimization over aggregate reentry risk th=
us conflicts with the deontological insistence that wrongs to individuals c=
annot be summed to justify violating one for the benefit of many. The use c=
ase=E2=80=99s lack of engagement with this paradox of deontology represents=
 a conceptual divergence.</p><p>The use case=E2=80=99s handling of conflict=
s of duties is also under-specified. Deontological theories recognize that =
categorical duties may conflict, requiring specification, prioritization, o=
r prima facie reasoning. The use case does not articulate how conflicts bet=
ween duties to individual households (e.g., not to harm) and duties to the =
system or community (e.g., to minimize reentries) are resolved. Instead, it=
 operationalizes a consequentialist compromise, which deontology would rega=
rd as insufficient without normative justification.</p><p>On the positive s=
ide, the use case=E2=80=99s empirical approach to identifying heterogeneity=
 in treatment effects and tailoring allocations to individual circumstances=
 aligns with deontology=E2=80=99s respect for persons as unique agents with=
 particular rights and needs. The use case=E2=80=99s recognition that some =
households benefit more from certain services and that allocations should r=
eflect this heterogeneity can be seen as respecting the individuality and a=
gency of persons, a core deontological concern.</p><p>In sum, the use case =
partially aligns with deontological principles in its attention to fairness=
 constraints, respect for human interpretability, and sensitivity to indivi=
dual heterogeneity. However, it diverges significantly in its foundational =
reliance on consequentialist optimization, its acceptance of harm to some i=
ndividuals for aggregate benefit without categorical justification, its und=
er-specification of consent and agency, and its lack of engagement with cor=
e deontological paradoxes and conflicts. These divergences are not merely t=
echnical but reflect intrinsic tensions between the use case=E2=80=99s cons=
equentialist methodology and deontology=E2=80=99s categorical normative com=
mitments.</p><h2>4) Stress Tests / Edge Cases</h2><p>To further probe the a=
lignment of the use case with deontological ethics, consider two stress tes=
t scenarios that reveal principled tensions or require fine-tuning.</p><p>F=
irst, imagine a scenario where the algorithm assigns a household with a sev=
ere disability and high vulnerability to a lower-intensity service that sta=
tistically increases their probability of reentry by a small margin, justif=
ied by the overall reduction in system-wide reentries. From a deontological=
 perspective, this allocation risks violating a categorical duty to protect=
 vulnerable persons from harm and to respect their rights to adequate care.=
 The use case=E2=80=99s fairness constraint might limit the increase in ree=
ntry risk, but it does not guarantee that the harm is morally permissible o=
r that the household=E2=80=99s consent is obtained. This scenario reveals a=
 principled flaw: the use case permits harm to individuals for aggregate be=
nefit without satisfying deontological prohibitions against using persons m=
erely as means or violating their rights. Addressing this would require exp=
licit categorical norms protecting vulnerable persons and mechanisms for ob=
taining or respecting consent, which the use case currently lacks.</p><p>Se=
cond, consider a scenario where two households have conflicting claims to a=
 scarce intensive service, and the algorithm must choose which to assign it=
 to minimize total reentries. The use case=E2=80=99s optimization will favo=
r the assignment that yields the greatest aggregate benefit, potentially vi=
olating the categorical duty not to harm or disadvantage one household for =
the sake of another. Deontology demands that such conflicts be resolved by =
specification of duties, prioritization of rights, or recognition of prima =
facie duties, not by aggregative calculation. The use case=E2=80=99s failur=
e to articulate such normative resolutions exposes a structural tension. Th=
is scenario calls for the integration of deontological conflict resolution =
mechanisms, such as specificationism or threshold deontology, to guide allo=
cation decisions beyond mere optimization.</p><p>These stress tests illustr=
ate that the use case=E2=80=99s consequentialist optimization framework is =
ill-equipped to handle core deontological demands regarding individual righ=
ts, agency, and categorical duties. While fairness constraints mitigate som=
e harms, they do not substitute for principled normative commitments. Minor=
 amendments, such as incorporating explicit categorical constraints and par=
ticipatory consent mechanisms, could improve alignment but may require stru=
ctural redesign to fully respect deontological norms.</p><h2>5) Recommendat=
ions</h2><p>In light of the foregoing evaluation, several recommendations e=
merge to enhance the alignment of the use case with deontological ethics or=
 to clarify the intrinsic limits of such alignment.</p><p>Minimal changes c=
ould include the explicit articulation and incorporation of categorical con=
straints that prohibit reallocations causing harm beyond a morally justifia=
ble threshold, not merely probabilistic increases in reentry risk. This wou=
ld require defining categorical duties to protect vulnerable households fro=
m harm and ensuring that algorithmic decisions respect these duties as invi=
olable constraints. Additionally, the use case should incorporate mechanism=
s for obtaining informed consent from households regarding the use of their=
 data and acceptance of reallocation decisions, thereby respecting their ag=
ency and rights.</p><p>Structural changes would involve redesigning the all=
ocation framework to integrate deontological conflict resolution methods, s=
uch as specificationism, to finely individuate duties and exceptions, or th=
e adoption of threshold deontology with clearly defined normative threshold=
s grounded in categorical norms rather than pragmatic trade-offs. This rede=
sign would shift the optimization from purely consequentialist aggregation =
to a hybrid model that respects categorical prohibitions and agent-relative=
 reasons, potentially at the cost of some efficiency but with greater moral=
 legitimacy.</p><p>Red lines for the use case, from the deontological stand=
point, include forbidding any allocation decision that knowingly violates a=
 categorical duty not to harm individuals or uses persons merely as means w=
ithout consent. The framework must ensure that no household is assigned to =
a service that increases their risk of harm beyond what is morally permissi=
ble, and that all decisions respect the separateness of persons and their a=
gent-relative rights.</p><p>Evaluation metrics should extend beyond aggrega=
te reentry rates to include measures of categorical norm compliance, respec=
t for individual rights, and the presence of informed consent. Empirical as=
sessments should examine whether any households are subjected to wrongful h=
arm or rights violations and whether the allocation process respects agent-=
relative reasons and permissions.</p><p>Ultimately, the use case=E2=80=99s =
foundational reliance on consequentialist optimization presents intrinsic m=
isalignment with deontological ethics. While pragmatic fairness constraints=
 and human oversight can mitigate some tensions, full alignment would requi=
re a normative reorientation that privileges categorical duties and rights =
over aggregate welfare. Such reorientation may limit the scope of algorithm=
ic optimization but would enhance the moral legitimacy and respect for pers=
ons that deontology demands.</p></div>
          </div>
