            <div class=3D"rendered-content"><h2>1) The Use-Case, Reconstruc=
ted</h2><p>The use-case under evaluation concerns the application of artifi=
cial intelligence (AI), machine learning (ML), and algorithmic decision-mak=
ing techniques to the allocation of scarce societal resources, specifically=
 homeless services in a metropolitan area of the United States. The decisio=
n problem centers on how to optimally allocate limited housing and support =
services to homeless households in a manner that minimizes the probability =
of reentry into the homeless system, thereby reducing overall demand and im=
proving system efficiency.</p><p>The proposed solution involves leveraging =
administrative data collected from a regional homeless management informati=
on system (HMIS) that records detailed household-level information, includi=
ng demographics, housing risk, service receipt, and hotline call history. T=
he core methodological innovation is the use of Bayesian Additive Regressio=
n Trees (BART), a machine learning model capable of estimating counterfactu=
al probabilities of reentry for each household under different service assi=
gnments. These counterfactual predictions enable the formulation of an inte=
ger programming optimization problem that assigns households to services so=
 as to minimize expected reentry rates subject to capacity constraints of e=
ach service type.</p><p>The target outcomes are primarily system-wide effic=
iency gains, operationalized as reductions in predicted and actual reentry =
rates into homelessness services within two years of exit. The study report=
s a predicted reduction of approximately 5.57% in reentry rates via optimiz=
ed allocation compared to historical assignments. However, this efficiency =
gain comes with trade-offs: about 21% of households are predicted to experi=
ence an increased probability of reentry under the optimized allocation, ra=
ising concerns about fairness and equity.</p><p>Implicit value claims embed=
ded in the use-case include the prioritization of aggregate welfare=E2=80=
=94measured as the overall reduction in homelessness recidivism=E2=80=94as =
the primary moral and policy goal. The approach assumes that maximizing the=
 total reduction in reentry is a legitimate and desirable objective, even i=
f it entails some individuals experiencing worse outcomes. The use-case als=
o implicitly values data-driven, evidence-based policy-making, emphasizing =
predictive accuracy, causal inference, and optimization under constraints a=
s means to improve social service delivery.</p><p>Stakeholders in this cont=
ext include homeless individuals and families seeking services, service pro=
viders and caseworkers, policymakers, and the broader community affected by=
 homelessness. The allocation mechanism privileges efficiency (minimizing r=
eentry) but recognizes the ethical complexity of fairness, as evidenced by =
the exploration of constrained optimization to limit harm to any individual=
 household.</p><p>Success in the use-case is defined quantitatively as the =
reduction in expected and observed reentry rates, with secondary considerat=
ion given to fairness constraints that limit increases in individual reentr=
y risk. The use-case assumes the availability and reliability of rich admin=
istrative data, the validity of counterfactual predictions from BART, and t=
he capacity to implement optimized allocations in practice. It also assumes=
 that reentry probability is a suitable proxy for service effectiveness and=
 social welfare.</p><p>In summary, the use-case presents a sophisticated, d=
ata-driven approach to a pressing social problem, aiming to optimize resour=
ce allocation to maximize aggregate welfare while grappling with fairness t=
rade-offs inherent in scarce resource distribution.</p><h2>2) The Framework=
, Reconstructed</h2><p>The ethical framework to be applied is classical uti=
litarianism, as elaborated in the detailed overview provided. Classical uti=
litarianism is a normative ethical theory grounded in consequentialism, hol=
ding that the moral status of actions depends solely on their consequences.=
 More specifically, classical utilitarianism is hedonistic, regarding pleas=
ure (happiness, well-being) as the only intrinsic good and pain (suffering,=
 unhappiness) as the only intrinsic bad. The theory is aggregative, summing=
 the pleasures and pains of all individuals affected to determine the overa=
ll utility of outcomes.</p><p>Key principles of classical utilitarianism in=
clude consequentialism, hedonism, aggregation, maximization (optimific prin=
ciple), impartiality, and inclusivity. Consequentialism requires that only =
outcomes matter morally; hedonism identifies pleasure as the sole intrinsic=
 good; aggregation sums individual utilities; maximization demands choosing=
 the action that produces the greatest total utility; impartiality requires=
 equal consideration of all individuals=E2=80=99 interests; inclusivity ext=
ends moral consideration to all sentient beings capable of pleasure or pain=
.</p><p>The framework=E2=80=99s scope is broad, applying to individual acti=
ons, social policies, and institutional decisions. It is committed to maxim=
izing overall happiness or well-being impartially and inclusively. The deci=
sion procedure, in principle, involves calculating the expected utility of =
available options and selecting the one with the highest total utility.</p>=
<p>Classical utilitarianism=E2=80=99s non-negotiables include the primacy o=
f utility maximization, the equal moral weight of all individuals=E2=80=99 =
interests, and the rejection of intrinsic moral constraints unrelated to co=
nsequences. Typical failure modes or objections include the demandingness o=
bjection (requiring excessive sacrifice), the separateness of persons objec=
tion (ignoring individual rights and justice), the =E2=80=9Cswine=E2=80=9D =
objection (reducing morality to base pleasures), and difficulties in practi=
cal decision procedures.</p><p>The framework recognizes the complexity of m=
easuring and aggregating utility, the challenges of impartiality, and the p=
otential conflicts between maximizing utility and respecting individual rig=
hts or fairness. It also acknowledges internal debates, such as act versus =
rule utilitarianism, qualitative distinctions among pleasures, and response=
s to demandingness.</p><p>In sum, classical utilitarianism demands that mor=
al evaluation and policy-making aim to maximize aggregate well-being impart=
ially, considering all affected individuals equally, and selecting actions =
or policies that produce the best overall consequences in terms of pleasure=
 and pain.</p><h2>3) Principle-by-Principle Deep Critical Evaluation</h2><p=
>The use-case=E2=80=99s alignment with classical utilitarianism can be crit=
ically examined by analyzing its adherence to the framework=E2=80=99s core =
principles: consequentialism, hedonism (or welfarism), aggregation, maximiz=
ation, impartiality, and inclusivity.</p><h3>Consequentialism</h3><p>The us=
e-case explicitly embraces consequentialism by focusing on the outcomes of =
service allocations, measured as probabilities of reentry into homelessness=
 services. The optimization seeks to minimize expected reentry rates, a pro=
xy for reducing suffering and improving well-being. This focus on outcomes =
rather than intrinsic features of actions or adherence to rules aligns well=
 with the consequentialist mandate.</p><p>However, the use-case operational=
izes consequences narrowly as reentry probabilities, which may not capture =
the full spectrum of well-being or suffering experienced by individuals. Wh=
ile reentry is a reasonable and pragmatic proxy for system effectiveness an=
d individual stability, classical utilitarianism requires a more comprehens=
ive account of pleasure and pain or well-being. The use-case does not expli=
citly justify this proxy or discuss other dimensions of well-being, such as=
 mental health, dignity, or autonomy, which may be affected by service assi=
gnments.</p><p>Thus, while the use-case adheres to the consequentialist pri=
nciple in form, it under-specifies the scope of consequences considered, po=
tentially limiting its fidelity to the framework=E2=80=99s substantive dema=
nds.</p><h3>Hedonism / Welfarism</h3><p>Classical utilitarianism=E2=80=99s =
hedonistic commitment identifies pleasure or happiness as the sole intrinsi=
c good. The use-case adopts a welfarist stance by aiming to improve well-be=
ing, operationalized as reducing homelessness recidivism. This is consisten=
t with the framework=E2=80=99s weaker welfarist interpretation, which allow=
s well-being to be measured in terms of objective indicators of life qualit=
y.</p><p>Nonetheless, the use-case does not explicitly engage with the natu=
re of well-being beyond reentry reduction. It does not address whether the =
reduction in reentry corresponds to increased happiness or decreased suffer=
ing in a hedonistic sense. Nor does it consider preference satisfaction or =
objective list theories of well-being. This omission leaves the use-case=E2=
=80=99s conception of utility underspecified relative to classical utilitar=
ianism=E2=80=99s demands.</p><p>Moreover, the use-case=E2=80=99s reliance o=
n administrative data and predictive modeling may obscure qualitative aspec=
ts of well-being, such as the subjective experience of individuals or the i=
ntrinsic value of social relationships. Without explicit consideration of t=
hese factors, the use-case risks a reductive interpretation of utility that=
 may diverge from classical utilitarianism=E2=80=99s richer hedonistic or w=
elfarist commitments.</p><h3>Aggregation</h3><p>The use-case embodies the a=
ggregative principle by summing predicted probabilities of reentry across h=
ouseholds and seeking to minimize the total expected reentry count. This re=
flects the classical utilitarian view that the moral value of outcomes is t=
he sum of individual utilities.</p><p>However, the use-case=E2=80=99s aggre=
gation is numerical and probabilistic, based on model predictions rather th=
an direct measures of individual utility or happiness. This introduces epis=
temic uncertainty and potential biases, which the use-case acknowledges but=
 does not fully resolve. The aggregation assumes commensurability and compa=
rability of individual reentry probabilities, which may not capture the qua=
litative differences in individuals=E2=80=99 experiences or the varying wei=
ghts that classical utilitarianism might assign to different persons=E2=80=
=99 well-being.</p><p>Furthermore, the use-case=E2=80=99s aggregation does =
not explicitly address the separateness of persons objection, which critiqu=
es utilitarianism for treating individuals as mere utility containers. The =
use-case=E2=80=99s optimization may sacrifice some individuals=E2=80=99 wel=
l-being (increased reentry risk) for aggregate gains, raising concerns abou=
t whether it respects the moral significance of individuals as distinct age=
nts.</p><h3>Maximization (Optimific Principle)</h3><p>The use-case=E2=80=99=
s optimization framework aligns closely with the classical utilitarian maxi=
mization requirement. It seeks the allocation that minimizes total expected=
 reentry, thereby maximizing aggregate welfare as defined.</p><p>Neverthele=
ss, the use-case reveals a tension with the maximization principle in pract=
ice. About 21% of households are assigned to services that increase their p=
redicted probability of reentry, indicating that the optimization accepts s=
ome individual harm for aggregate benefit. Classical utilitarianism mandate=
s maximizing total utility without exception, which implies no room for ind=
ividual detriment if it reduces overall utility.</p><p>The use-case attempt=
s to address this tension by introducing constrained optimization that limi=
ts individual increases in reentry probability, trading off some efficiency=
 for fairness. This move aligns with critiques of classical utilitarianism=
=E2=80=99s demandingness and insensitivity to individual harm but departs f=
rom strict maximization.</p><p>This divergence reflects a pragmatic adaptat=
ion rather than pure classical utilitarianism. It acknowledges the moral an=
d political costs of sacrificing individuals for aggregate gains, which cla=
ssical utilitarianism struggles to accommodate without modification.</p><h3=
>Impartiality</h3><p>Classical utilitarianism requires impartial considerat=
ion of all individuals=E2=80=99 interests, treating each person=E2=80=99s w=
ell-being as equally important. The use-case=E2=80=99s aggregation and opti=
mization procedures embody this impartiality by summing reentry probabiliti=
es across all households without privileging any subgroup.</p><p>The use-ca=
se=E2=80=99s data-driven approach treats all households as morally equal un=
its in the optimization, consistent with the framework=E2=80=99s egalitaria=
nism. It does not prioritize based on identity, status, or proximity, refle=
cting the utilitarian ideal of universal concern.</p><p>However, the use-ca=
se=E2=80=99s fairness analysis reveals that some households benefit more th=
an others, and some are harmed, raising questions about whether the optimiz=
ation respects the equal moral importance of all individuals. The constrain=
ed optimization attempts to mitigate this by limiting harm, but it does not=
 fully resolve the tension between impartial maximization and individual ri=
ghts or claims.</p><p>Moreover, the use-case does not explicitly discuss ho=
w to weigh competing claims or address potential conflicts between impartia=
lity and other moral considerations, such as special duties or rights, whic=
h classical utilitarianism generally rejects but which are salient in socia=
l policy.</p><h3>Inclusivity</h3><p>The framework demands inclusivity in mo=
ral consideration, extending to all sentient beings capable of pleasure or =
pain. The use-case focuses exclusively on human homeless households, implic=
itly excluding non-human animals or other sentient beings from consideratio=
n.</p><p>While this limitation is understandable given the social policy co=
ntext, it represents a divergence from the framework=E2=80=99s inclusivity =
principle. The use-case does not justify this exclusion or discuss its ethi=
cal implications.</p><p>Furthermore, the use-case=E2=80=99s reliance on adm=
inistrative data may exclude marginalized or unrecorded populations, raisin=
g concerns about the inclusivity of the data and the resulting allocations.=
 The framework would require careful attention to ensure that all morally r=
elevant individuals are included in the welfare calculations.</p><h3>Additi=
onal Considerations: Fairness, Rights, and Justice</h3><p>Classical utilita=
rianism=E2=80=99s focus on aggregate utility often conflicts with notions o=
f justice, rights, and fairness. The use-case explicitly grapples with fair=
ness concerns, acknowledging that some households are harmed by the optimiz=
ed allocation and exploring constrained optimization to limit individual ha=
rm.</p><p>This recognition of fairness trade-offs aligns with broader utili=
tarian debates about demandingness and the separateness of persons. However=
, classical utilitarianism does not provide intrinsic protections for indiv=
idual rights or justice beyond their instrumental contribution to utility m=
aximization.</p><p>The use-case=E2=80=99s constrained optimization represen=
ts a pragmatic modification that reflects these concerns but departs from s=
trict classical utilitarianism. It introduces a form of rule-like constrain=
t to protect individuals from excessive harm, resembling rule utilitarianis=
m or other consequentialist variants.</p><p>The use-case does not explicitl=
y discuss rights or justice concepts, nor does it provide a principled just=
ification for the fairness constraints beyond pragmatic considerations. Thi=
s omission leaves a gap relative to the framework=E2=80=99s engagement with=
 these perennial challenges.</p><h3>Transparency and Decision Procedure</h3=
><p>Classical utilitarianism demands a decision procedure based on calculat=
ing expected utilities. The use-case operationalizes this through BART pred=
ictions and integer programming optimization, providing a rigorous, data-dr=
iven decision procedure.</p><p>This methodological sophistication aligns wi=
th the framework=E2=80=99s emphasis on rational calculation of consequences=
. However, the use-case acknowledges challenges in prediction accuracy, dat=
a limitations, and model biases, which introduce epistemic uncertainty.</p>=
<p>The framework requires that moral decisions be based on the best availab=
le evidence and reasoning, but the use-case=E2=80=99s reliance on complex m=
odels may obscure transparency and interpretability, raising concerns about=
 accountability and trust.</p><h3>Summary of Alignment and Divergence</h3><=
p>Overall, the use-case aligns strongly with classical utilitarianism=E2=80=
=99s consequentialist, aggregative, impartial, and maximizing principles in=
 form and intent. It operationalizes these principles through sophisticated=
 predictive modeling and optimization to improve aggregate welfare.</p><p>H=
owever, it diverges or remains under-justified in several respects: the nar=
row operationalization of well-being as reentry probability; the acceptance=
 of individual harm for aggregate gain; limited engagement with the qualita=
tive nature of pleasure and pain; exclusion of non-human interests; and the=
 pragmatic introduction of fairness constraints without full theoretical gr=
ounding.</p><p>These divergences reflect intrinsic tensions in applying cla=
ssical utilitarianism to complex social policy problems, where trade-offs b=
etween aggregate welfare and individual fairness are unavoidable.</p><h2>4)=
 Stress Tests / Edge Cases</h2><p>To further probe the use-case=E2=80=99s a=
lignment with classical utilitarianism, consider the following scenarios:</=
p><h3>Scenario 1: The =E2=80=9CSacrificial Household=E2=80=9D</h3><p>Imagin=
e a household that, under the optimized allocation, is assigned to a servic=
e predicted to increase their probability of reentry by a significant margi=
n, while this assignment substantially reduces reentry probabilities for ma=
ny other households. This household suffers a markedly worse outcome for th=
e sake of aggregate efficiency.</p><p>Classical utilitarianism, in its stri=
ct form, would endorse this sacrifice as morally required, since maximizing=
 total utility trumps individual harm. The use-case=E2=80=99s unconstrained=
 optimization reflects this logic.</p><p>However, this scenario exposes a m=
oral tension: the household=E2=80=99s increased suffering is real and signi=
ficant, raising questions about justice and rights. The use-case attempts t=
o address this by imposing constraints limiting harm, but this reduces effi=
ciency gains.</p><p>This edge case reveals an intrinsic conflict between cl=
assical utilitarianism=E2=80=99s maximization and individual fairness, sugg=
esting that the use-case=E2=80=99s pragmatic fairness constraints are neces=
sary but represent a departure from pure classical utilitarianism.</p><h3>S=
cenario 2: The =E2=80=9CData-Excluded Household=E2=80=9D</h3><p>Consider a =
homeless individual or family not captured in the administrative data due t=
o systemic biases, lack of access, or data errors. This household is exclud=
ed from the optimization and thus from equitable consideration in resource =
allocation.</p><p>Classical utilitarianism requires inclusivity of all sent=
ient beings=E2=80=99 interests. Excluding individuals from the welfare calc=
ulation violates this principle, potentially leading to unjust outcomes and=
 undermining impartiality.</p><p>The use-case does not explicitly address d=
ata completeness or mechanisms to include marginalized or unrecorded popula=
tions. This omission risks perpetuating inequities and misaligns with the f=
ramework=E2=80=99s inclusivity and impartiality demands.</p><p>This scenari=
o stresses the importance of comprehensive, unbiased data and ethical safeg=
uards to ensure all morally relevant individuals are considered.</p><h2>5) =
Recommendations</h2><p>In light of the above evaluation, several recommenda=
tions emerge to improve alignment with classical utilitarianism:</p><p>Firs=
t, the use-case should explicitly articulate and justify its conception of =
well-being beyond reentry probability, incorporating broader dimensions of =
pleasure, pain, and life quality consistent with hedonistic or welfarist co=
mmitments. This would strengthen the moral grounding of the optimization ob=
jective.</p><p>Second, the use-case should transparently address the episte=
mic uncertainties inherent in predictive modeling and aggregation, includin=
g potential biases and limitations, to uphold the framework=E2=80=99s deman=
d for rational, evidence-based decision-making.</p><p>Third, the fairness c=
onstraints introduced to limit individual harm should be theoretically just=
ified within a utilitarian framework, perhaps by invoking rule utilitariani=
sm or scalar utilitarianism variants, to reconcile efficiency with fairness=
 without abandoning utilitarian principles.</p><p>Fourth, the use-case shou=
ld explicitly consider inclusivity by ensuring data completeness and mechan=
isms to incorporate marginalized or unrecorded populations, thereby respect=
ing the framework=E2=80=99s impartiality and inclusivity.</p><p>Fifth, the =
use-case should engage with classical utilitarianism=E2=80=99s challenges r=
egarding the separateness of persons and the potential conflict between agg=
regate welfare and individual rights, providing a principled account of how=
 these tensions are managed in practice.</p><p>Finally, the use-case should=
 enhance transparency and interpretability of the algorithmic decision proc=
edure to foster trust, accountability, and ethical legitimacy, consistent w=
ith utilitarianism=E2=80=99s concern for overall well-being including socia=
l trust.</p><p>While some divergences reflect intrinsic tensions between cl=
assical utilitarianism and practical social policy, these recommendations a=
im to deepen the use-case=E2=80=99s theoretical coherence and ethical robus=
tness within the framework.</p></div>
