            <div class=3D"rendered-content"><h2>1) The Use-Case, Reconstruc=
ted</h2><p>The use-case under consideration is a data-driven, algorithmic a=
pproach to the allocation of scarce homeless services within a metropolitan=
 area in the United States. The decision problem it addresses is the optima=
l distribution of limited housing and support resources among homeless hous=
eholds to minimize the probability of reentry into the homeless system with=
in two years. The proposed mechanism combines machine learning predictive m=
odels=E2=80=94specifically Bayesian Additive Regression Trees (BART)=E2=80=
=94to estimate counterfactual probabilities of reentry for each household u=
nder different service assignments, with an integer programming optimizatio=
n framework to allocate services subject to capacity constraints.</p><p>The=
 target outcomes are twofold: first, to improve the overall efficiency of t=
he homeless service system by reducing the expected number of households wh=
o return to homelessness; second, to consider fairness and equity in alloca=
tion, balancing efficiency gains with the risk that some households may be =
assigned to services that increase their probability of reentry. The moral =
rationale explicitly foregrounds the tension between efficiency (maximizing=
 the overall benefit of scarce resources) and fairness (ensuring that no ho=
usehold is unduly harmed by reallocation). The authors acknowledge the ethi=
cal complexities inherent in algorithmic decision-making in social services=
, including concerns about bias, transparency, and accountability.</p><p>Im=
plicit value claims in the use-case include the prioritization of reducing =
homelessness recidivism as a social good, the assumption that data-driven p=
redictive accuracy can meaningfully improve social outcomes, and that fairn=
ess can be operationalized as constraints on individual increases in predic=
ted risk. The use-case assumes that the predictive model=E2=80=99s counterf=
actual estimates are sufficiently reliable to guide allocation decisions an=
d that capacity constraints realistically reflect service availability. Suc=
cess is measured quantitatively by reductions in predicted and actual reent=
ry rates, and qualitatively by the extent to which fairness constraints mit=
igate harms to individual households.</p><p>Stakeholders include homeless i=
ndividuals and families seeking services, service providers and caseworkers=
, policymakers, and the broader community affected by homelessness. The use=
-case privileges system-wide efficiency while attempting to safeguard indiv=
idual fairness, implicitly endorsing a utilitarian calculus tempered by con=
straints to protect vulnerable individuals from disproportionate harm. The =
data used are administrative records linked across services and time, with =
a rich set of demographic and service-related features. The use-case assume=
s that the administrative data are accurate and comprehensive, and that the=
 counterfactual modeling adequately addresses confounding in observational =
data.</p><p>In sum, the use-case proposes a sophisticated, data-driven poli=
cy tool to allocate scarce homeless services, aiming to optimize social wel=
fare by reducing homelessness recidivism while incorporating fairness const=
raints to protect individual interests. It situates itself within ongoing d=
ebates about the ethical use of AI in social policy, emphasizing the need t=
o balance efficiency, equity, and transparency.</p><h2>2) The Framework, Re=
constructed</h2><p>The ethical framework employed for evaluation is Rawlsia=
n Justice, as articulated in the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry =
by Leif Wenar. Rawlsian Justice is a political philosophy grounded in the l=
iberal tradition, emphasizing justice as fairness within a society of free =
and equal citizens cooperating under just institutions. The framework is st=
ructured around several core principles and methodological commitments.</p>=
<p>At its normative core, Rawlsian Justice prioritizes the equal basic libe=
rties of all citizens, which are indefeasible and must be guaranteed equall=
y. Beyond liberties, it demands fair equality of opportunity and permits so=
cial and economic inequalities only if they benefit the least advantaged (t=
he difference principle). The framework conceives of society as a fair syst=
em of cooperation among free and equal citizens, where justice is the virtu=
e of social institutions distributing benefits and burdens fairly.</p><p>Ra=
wlsian Justice is deeply concerned with legitimacy and stability in a plura=
listic society marked by reasonable pluralism=E2=80=94diverse worldviews th=
at citizens reasonably hold. Legitimate political power must be exercised a=
ccording to principles that all reasonable citizens can endorse, and stabil=
ity arises from an overlapping consensus where diverse comprehensive doctri=
nes converge on a shared political conception of justice.</p><p>Methodologi=
cally, Rawls employs the original position and veil of ignorance as a thoug=
ht experiment to derive principles of justice that free and equal citizens =
would agree to under fair conditions. This construct ensures impartiality b=
y depriving parties of knowledge of their own social status, talents, or pa=
rticular circumstances. The framework distinguishes ideal theory (assuming =
full compliance and favorable conditions) from non-ideal theory (addressing=
 violations and unfavorable conditions), emphasizing that ideal theory sets=
 the standard for just institutions.</p><p>Rawlsian Justice treats the basi=
c structure of society=E2=80=94the major political, social, and economic in=
stitutions=E2=80=94as the primary subject of justice, since these instituti=
ons shape life prospects and social status. Justice requires publicity and =
transparency of principles, and political power must be exercised in accord=
ance with public reason, meaning justifications must appeal to shared polit=
ical values accessible to all citizens.</p><p>The framework=E2=80=99s non-n=
egotiables include the priority of equal basic liberties over other social =
goods, the requirement of fair equality of opportunity, and the difference =
principle=E2=80=99s strict conditions on inequalities. It rejects utilitari=
anism=E2=80=99s aggregation of welfare at the expense of individual rights =
and insists on reciprocity and mutual respect among citizens.</p><p>Typical=
 failure modes of Rawlsian Justice include ignoring the equal basic liberti=
es in favor of aggregate welfare, failing to secure fair equality of opport=
unity, permitting unjust inequalities, or relying on comprehensive doctrine=
s rather than freestanding political conceptions of justice. The framework =
is also sensitive to the risk that institutions fail to secure stability or=
 legitimacy due to lack of overlapping consensus or public reason.</p><p>Cr=
itiques of Rawlsian Justice often focus on its idealizing assumptions, pote=
ntial under-specification of non-ideal theory, and challenges in operationa=
lizing the difference principle. However, its strength lies in providing a =
principled, coherent account of justice that balances liberty and equality =
within a pluralistic democratic society.</p><p>In sum, Rawlsian Justice dem=
ands that social institutions guarantee equal basic liberties, ensure fair =
equality of opportunity, and arrange inequalities to benefit the least adva=
ntaged, all within a framework of legitimacy, stability, and public reason.=
 Justice is conceived as fairness in the basic structure, justified through=
 the original position and reflective equilibrium.</p><h2>3) Principle-by-P=
rinciple Deep Critical Evaluation</h2><p>The evaluation of the use-case thr=
ough the lens of Rawlsian Justice requires a detailed examination of how th=
e proposed algorithmic allocation of homeless services aligns or diverges f=
rom the framework=E2=80=99s core principles: equal basic liberties, fair eq=
uality of opportunity, the difference principle, legitimacy and stability t=
hrough public reason and overlapping consensus, and the publicity and fairn=
ess of institutions.</p><h3>Equal Basic Liberties and Political Equality</h=
3><p>Rawls=E2=80=99s first principle demands that all citizens have equal a=
nd indefeasible basic liberties, including political liberties and rights t=
o fair treatment under just institutions. While the use-case does not expli=
citly address political liberties, it concerns a fundamental social good=E2=
=80=94access to housing and social services=E2=80=94that is closely tied to=
 citizens=E2=80=99 capacity to exercise their liberties and participate as =
equals in society.</p><p>The use-case=E2=80=99s focus on reducing homelessn=
ess recidivism can be interpreted as supporting the conditions necessary fo=
r citizens to enjoy their basic liberties. Stable housing is a prerequisite=
 for effective participation in political and social life. However, the use=
-case does not explicitly frame access to homeless services as a matter of =
equal basic rights or liberties, nor does it address whether the allocation=
 mechanism respects or enhances political equality.</p><p>Moreover, Rawls=
=E2=80=99s framework requires that political liberties have fair value, mea=
ning that citizens have not only formal but substantive equality in politic=
al participation. The use-case does not engage with political equality or t=
he mechanisms by which homeless individuals might influence or consent to a=
llocation decisions. The algorithmic allocation is a top-down mechanism, wi=
th no explicit provision for democratic participation or consent by the aff=
ected households.</p><p>This absence raises concerns about legitimacy under=
 Rawlsian Justice. The liberal principle of legitimacy requires that coerci=
ve power be exercised in accordance with principles all citizens can reason=
ably endorse. The use-case does not specify whether the allocation mechanis=
m is publicly justified or whether affected individuals have reason to endo=
rse it. The use-case=E2=80=99s reliance on administrative data and algorith=
mic decision-making risks alienating those served if they perceive the proc=
ess as opaque or imposed without their input.</p><p>In sum, while the use-c=
ase aims to improve conditions that support basic liberties, it falls short=
 of Rawlsian demands for political equality and legitimacy. It lacks explic=
it mechanisms for public reason or democratic endorsement, which are necess=
ary for the legitimate exercise of coercive power in a liberal society.</p>=
<h3>Fair Equality of Opportunity</h3><p>Rawls=E2=80=99s second principle pr=
ioritizes fair equality of opportunity, requiring that citizens with simila=
r talents and willingness have equal prospects regardless of morally arbitr=
ary factors such as social class or background. The use-case=E2=80=99s deta=
iled data collection and modeling of individual characteristics align with =
the Rawlsian concern to neutralize the effects of arbitrary social continge=
ncies.</p><p>By estimating counterfactual probabilities of reentry for each=
 household under different service assignments, the use-case attempts to ta=
ilor allocations to individual needs and circumstances, potentially improvi=
ng fair opportunity outcomes. The use-case=E2=80=99s attention to heterogen=
eity in treatment effects and its use of rich demographic and service data =
suggest a commitment to recognizing individual differences rather than appl=
ying uniform rules.</p><p>However, Rawlsian Justice demands more than recog=
nition of individual differences; it requires that institutions actively co=
mpensate for social contingencies that undermine equal opportunity. The use=
-case does not explicitly address whether the allocation algorithm corrects=
 for structural inequalities or redistributes resources to compensate for d=
isadvantages. The optimization objective is to minimize overall reentry, wh=
ich may or may not align with advancing fair equality of opportunity for th=
e worst-off.</p><p>The use-case=E2=80=99s fairness constraints=E2=80=94limi=
ting increases in individual predicted risk=E2=80=94reflect a sensitivity t=
o individual harms but do not guarantee that disadvantaged groups receive e=
nhanced opportunities. The analysis shows that some vulnerable households b=
enefit disproportionately, which is promising, but the use-case does not sy=
stematically incorporate the difference principle=E2=80=99s requirement tha=
t inequalities benefit the least advantaged.</p><p>Furthermore, Rawlsian fa=
ir equality of opportunity entails substantive equality in access to politi=
cal and social offices, education, and economic opportunities. The use-case=
 focuses narrowly on housing services and does not engage with broader soci=
al institutions that shape opportunity. Its scope is thus limited relative =
to Rawls=E2=80=99s comprehensive conception of fair equality of opportunity=
.</p><p>Overall, the use-case partially aligns with Rawlsian fair equality =
of opportunity by recognizing individual heterogeneity and attempting to im=
prove allocations accordingly. Yet it lacks an explicit commitment to compe=
nsatory justice or prioritization of the least advantaged beyond minimizing=
 reentry risk, leaving a gap in fully realizing Rawlsian equality.</p><h3>T=
he Difference Principle</h3><p>The difference principle allows social and e=
conomic inequalities only if they benefit the least advantaged members of s=
ociety. It is a distinctive hallmark of Rawlsian Justice, emphasizing recip=
rocity and social unity. The use-case=E2=80=99s objective to minimize homel=
essness recidivism can be interpreted as advancing the interests of the lea=
st advantaged, since homelessness is a severe form of social disadvantage.<=
/p><p>The use-case=E2=80=99s empirical findings that the optimal allocation=
 disproportionately benefits more vulnerable households=E2=80=94those homel=
ess upon entry, with disabilities, or frequent hotline calls=E2=80=94sugges=
t that the algorithm may realize aspects of the difference principle in pra=
ctice. This is a notable strength, indicating that data-driven allocation c=
an potentially promote social reciprocity and improve outcomes for the wors=
t-off.</p><p>However, the use-case does not explicitly frame its optimizati=
on or fairness constraints in Rawlsian terms, nor does it provide a normati=
ve justification grounded in the difference principle. The objective functi=
on minimizes aggregate reentry risk, which is a utilitarian-style aggregate=
 measure rather than a maximin criterion focusing on the worst-off. The fai=
rness constraints limit harm but do not guarantee that inequalities in serv=
ice allocation maximize the position of the least advantaged.</p><p>Moreove=
r, Rawlsian Justice requires that inequalities be attached to offices and p=
ositions open to all under fair equality of opportunity. The use-case does =
not address whether access to services is open and fair in this sense, nor =
does it consider the political or social status implications of service all=
ocations. The use-case=E2=80=99s reliance on administrative data and algori=
thmic decisions may obscure or entrench existing power asymmetries, potenti=
ally conflicting with the reciprocity Rawls demands.</p><p>In addition, Raw=
ls=E2=80=99s difference principle is embedded in a broader theory of justic=
e that includes equal basic liberties and public reason. The use-case=E2=80=
=99s focus on efficiency and constrained fairness does not engage with thes=
e broader institutional requirements, limiting its alignment with the full =
Rawlsian ideal.</p><p>Thus, while the use-case=E2=80=99s outcomes may appro=
ximate the difference principle=E2=80=99s aim of benefiting the least advan=
taged, its design and justification do not fully embody Rawlsian reciprocit=
y or the normative priority of the worst-off.</p><h3>Legitimacy, Stability,=
 and Public Reason</h3><p>Rawlsian Justice places paramount importance on l=
egitimacy=E2=80=94the rightful exercise of political power=E2=80=94and stab=
ility=E2=80=94the willing compliance of citizens. Legitimacy requires that =
political power be exercised according to principles all reasonable citizen=
s can endorse, and that public reason guide political justification.</p><p>=
The use-case involves algorithmic decision-making that affects individuals=
=E2=80=99 access to essential social services, a form of coercive power ins=
ofar as it shapes life opportunities and may exclude or disadvantage some h=
ouseholds. From a Rawlsian perspective, such power must be exercised legiti=
mately, with transparent, publicly justifiable principles.</p><p>The use-ca=
se acknowledges ethical concerns about fairness, transparency, and accounta=
bility, and discusses constraints to limit harm. However, it does not speci=
fy how the allocation mechanism is publicly justified or how affected indiv=
iduals participate in or consent to the decision process. The opacity of ma=
chine learning models and the complexity of counterfactual inference pose c=
hallenges for public reason, as citizens may not understand or endorse the =
rationale for allocations.</p><p>Rawls=E2=80=99s doctrine of public reason =
requires that political decisions be justified by shared political values a=
ccessible to all citizens. The use-case=E2=80=99s reliance on predictive mo=
dels and optimization objectives grounded in statistical risk reduction doe=
s not obviously translate into public political values or reasons that all =
citizens can reasonably accept. The absence of explicit engagement with pub=
lic reason or overlapping consensus raises questions about the legitimacy o=
f the allocation mechanism.</p><p>Stability in Rawlsian terms arises from o=
verlapping consensus, where diverse citizens endorse the political concepti=
on of justice from their own perspectives. The use-case does not address wh=
ether the allocation mechanism enjoys such consensus or whether it might pr=
ovoke resistance or mistrust among those disadvantaged by reallocations.</p=
><p>Furthermore, Rawls emphasizes the publicity and transparency of just in=
stitutions. The use-case=E2=80=99s algorithmic approach, while data-driven =
and empirically grounded, risks being perceived as a black box, undermining=
 the publicity condition. Although the authors discuss the possibility of w=
orker overrides and human interpretability, these are not fully integrated =
into the allocation mechanism or its justification.</p><p>In sum, the use-c=
ase falls short of Rawlsian demands for legitimacy and stability grounded i=
n public reason and overlapping consensus. Without mechanisms for public ju=
stification, transparency, and democratic participation, the algorithmic al=
location risks being an illegitimate exercise of coercive power, underminin=
g social stability.</p><h3>Publicity and Fairness of Institutions</h3><p>Ra=
wlsian Justice requires that the principles and operations of just institut=
ions be publicly known and accessible, avoiding ideological concealment or =
domination. The use-case=E2=80=99s deployment of complex machine learning m=
odels and optimization algorithms challenges this requirement.</p><p>While =
the use-case demonstrates empirical rigor and sensitivity to fairness, it d=
oes not provide mechanisms to ensure that the allocation process is underst=
andable and justifiable to the public or to the households served. The opac=
ity of BART models and integer programming solutions may hinder meaningful =
public scrutiny.</p><p>The use-case=E2=80=99s discussion of fairness constr=
aints and potential for worker overrides is promising, suggesting some inst=
itutional checks. However, these features are not fully elaborated or insti=
tutionalized, leaving open the risk that algorithmic decisions may be perce=
ived as arbitrary or unaccountable.</p><p>Moreover, Rawlsian fairness invol=
ves more than minimizing harm; it requires that institutions embody recipro=
city, respect, and equal concern. The use-case=E2=80=99s focus on predicted=
 probabilities and optimization objectives may obscure these normative dime=
nsions.</p><p>Therefore, while the use-case advances fairness in a technica=
l sense, it does not fully realize Rawlsian publicity and fairness of insti=
tutions, risking erosion of trust and legitimacy.</p><h3>Independence of Mo=
ral and Political Theory</h3><p>Rawlsian Justice distinguishes political co=
nceptions of justice from comprehensive moral or religious doctrines, empha=
sizing freestanding political principles. The use-case operates within a pr=
agmatic, empirical domain, focusing on social service delivery rather than =
metaphysical or moral theory.</p><p>This pragmatic orientation aligns with =
Rawls=E2=80=99s methodological presumption that political theory proceeds l=
argely independent of metaphysics. The use-case=E2=80=99s reliance on data =
and predictive modeling is consistent with Rawls=E2=80=99s view that politi=
cal philosophy can be practical and utopian within reasonable limits.</p><p=
>However, Rawlsian Justice also requires that political principles be justi=
fiable to citizens as free and equal persons. The use-case=E2=80=99s techni=
cal focus may limit its capacity to engage with the normative reasoning cit=
izens require to endorse the allocation mechanism.</p><p>In this respect, t=
he use-case aligns with Rawls=E2=80=99s independence of political theory bu=
t diverges in its limited engagement with the justificatory demands of poli=
tical liberalism.</p><h3>Summary of Principle-by-Principle Evaluation</h3><=
p>The use-case exhibits partial alignment with Rawlsian Justice in its empi=
rical attention to individual heterogeneity, efforts to reduce disadvantage=
, and sensitivity to fairness constraints. It advances social cooperation b=
y seeking to improve outcomes for vulnerable populations, resonating with t=
he difference principle=E2=80=99s spirit.</p><p>However, significant diverg=
ences arise in the absence of explicit commitments to equal basic liberties=
, political equality, legitimacy through public reason, and institutional p=
ublicity. The algorithmic allocation mechanism lacks democratic endorsement=
, transparency, and mechanisms for overlapping consensus, undermining Rawls=
ian stability and legitimacy.</p><p>The use-case=E2=80=99s optimization obj=
ective is utilitarian in form, focusing on aggregate risk minimization rath=
er than maximin principles prioritizing the worst-off. Fairness constraints=
 mitigate harms but do not fully realize Rawlsian reciprocity or fair equal=
ity of opportunity.</p><p>In sum, the use-case is a technically sophisticat=
ed and ethically aware proposal that partially realizes Rawlsian ideals but=
 falls short of fully embodying the framework=E2=80=99s normative commitmen=
ts to legitimacy, political equality, and public reason.</p><h2>4) Stress T=
ests / Edge Cases</h2><p>To further probe the use-case=E2=80=99s alignment =
with Rawlsian Justice, consider the following scenarios:</p><h3>Scenario 1:=
 A Household with Limited Political Voice is Assigned a Service Increasing =
Reentry Risk</h3><p>Suppose a homeless household belonging to a marginalize=
d racial minority with limited political influence is assigned, by the algo=
rithm, to a service that increases their predicted probability of reentry b=
y just under the fairness constraint threshold. This household lacks meanin=
gful avenues to contest or influence the allocation decision.</p><p>Under R=
awlsian Justice, this raises serious concerns. The household=E2=80=99s equa=
l basic liberties and fair equality of opportunity are compromised if they =
are systematically disadvantaged by opaque algorithmic decisions without re=
course. The absence of public reason and democratic participation in the al=
location process undermines legitimacy. The overlapping consensus is fragil=
e if vulnerable groups perceive the system as unjust or unresponsive.</p><p=
>This scenario reveals a principled flaw: the use-case=E2=80=99s fairness c=
onstraints, while limiting harm, do not guarantee protection of the least a=
dvantaged or political equality. Without mechanisms for participation, tran=
sparency, or appeal, the allocation risks violating Rawlsian legitimacy and=
 stability.</p><h3>Scenario 2: Public Backlash Against Algorithmic Allocati=
on Undermines Social Stability</h3><p>Imagine that the public, including se=
rvice users and advocates, learns that the allocation algorithm reallocates=
 some households to services that increase their risk of homelessness. The =
complexity and opacity of the algorithm prevent meaningful understanding or=
 challenge. Public trust erodes, leading to protests and resistance to the =
homeless service system.</p><p>Rawlsian Justice emphasizes that stability a=
rises from overlapping consensus and public reason. This scenario illustrat=
es how failure to secure legitimacy through transparent, publicly justifiab=
le institutions can destabilize social cooperation. The use-case=E2=80=99s =
lack of explicit mechanisms for public engagement or justification risks su=
ch outcomes.</p><p>This edge case underscores the necessity of embedding al=
gorithmic allocation within institutions that ensure publicity, accountabil=
ity, and democratic legitimacy, as Rawls requires.</p><h3>Scenario 3: Struc=
tural Inequalities Are Entrenched by Algorithmic Bias</h3><p>Consider that =
the administrative data used to train the predictive model reflect historic=
al biases and structural inequalities, such as racial discrimination in ser=
vice provision. The algorithm learns and perpetuates these biases, systemat=
ically disadvantaging certain groups.</p><p>Rawlsian Justice demands that i=
nstitutions compensate for arbitrary social contingencies and not perpetuat=
e injustice. The use-case=E2=80=99s reliance on observational data without =
explicit correction for bias risks violating the difference principle and f=
air equality of opportunity.</p><p>This scenario reveals a critical vulnera=
bility: the use-case=E2=80=99s technical sophistication does not guarantee =
normative justice if data biases are unaddressed. It calls for explicit mec=
hanisms to detect and correct bias to align with Rawlsian demands.</p><h2>5=
) Recommendations</h2><p>In light of the evaluation, several recommendation=
s emerge to improve alignment of the use-case with Rawlsian Justice:</p><p>=
First, the allocation mechanism should be embedded within a publicly accoun=
table institutional framework that ensures legitimacy through public reason=
. This includes transparent communication of allocation principles, opportu=
nities for affected households to understand and contest decisions, and dem=
ocratic oversight. Without such mechanisms, the exercise of coercive power =
risks illegitimacy and instability.</p><p>Second, the use-case should expli=
citly incorporate Rawlsian normative priorities in its objective function a=
nd constraints. Rather than minimizing aggregate reentry risk alone, the op=
timization should prioritize the worst-off households consistent with the d=
ifference principle, ensuring that inequalities in service allocation benef=
it the least advantaged. This may require adopting maximin criteria or weig=
hted objectives reflecting social reciprocity.</p><p>Third, the algorithmic=
 model must actively address structural biases in the data. Techniques for =
bias detection, fairness-aware machine learning, and ongoing audit should b=
e integrated to prevent perpetuation of historical injustices. This aligns =
with Rawls=E2=80=99s demand to neutralize morally arbitrary contingencies a=
nd promote fair equality of opportunity.</p><p>Fourth, the design should in=
corporate mechanisms for political equality and participation. This could i=
nclude involving service users and community representatives in the design,=
 evaluation, and governance of the allocation system, thereby fostering ove=
rlapping consensus and stability.</p><p>Fifth, the use-case should enhance =
publicity and transparency by developing interpretable models or explanatio=
ns accessible to non-expert stakeholders. This supports the Rawlsian public=
ity condition and helps build trust.</p><p>Finally, fairness constraints sh=
ould be refined to ensure that no household is assigned to a service that s=
ignificantly worsens their position, consistent with reciprocity and mutual=
 respect. These constraints should be coupled with remedies or compensation=
s for those adversely affected, to uphold justice as fairness.</p><p>In con=
clusion, while the use-case offers a promising data-driven approach to impr=
oving homeless service allocation, substantial revisions are necessary to f=
ully realize Rawlsian Justice. Embedding the algorithm within just institut=
ions that secure equal basic liberties, fair equality of opportunity, the d=
ifference principle, legitimacy through public reason, and publicity is ess=
ential for ethical acceptability and social stability.</p></div>
