            <div class=3D"rendered-content"><h1>Framework Evaluation of Fai=
r and Efficient Allocation of Scarce Resources Based on Predicted Outcomes:=
 Implications for Homeless Service Delivery via Contractualism</h1><h2>1) T=
he Use-Case, Reconstructed</h2><p>The use case under evaluation addresses t=
he allocation of scarce resources within the context of homelessness servic=
es in the United States. It proposes an algorithmic approach to optimize th=
e assignment of households in need to various types of homeless assistance,=
 including emergency shelters, transitional housing, rapid rehousing, and h=
omelessness prevention. The decision problem centers on how to effectively =
allocate limited resources to maximize the efficiency of service delivery w=
hile minimizing the likelihood of households re-entering the homeless syste=
m.</p><p>The proposed mechanism leverages machine learning, specifically Ba=
yesian Additive Regression Trees (BART), to predict the probability of re-e=
ntry into the homeless system for each household based on their assigned se=
rvice. By analyzing historical data, the algorithm aims to determine which =
service would yield the best outcomes for individual households, thereby op=
timizing the allocation process. The stated moral rationale is to improve t=
he overall efficiency of the homeless service system while addressing the p=
ressing needs of vulnerable populations.</p><p>Key stakeholders in this sce=
nario include homeless service providers, policymakers, and the households =
seeking assistance. The optimization process is designed to privilege those=
 households that are most likely to benefit from specific services, thereby=
 enhancing the overall effectiveness of resource allocation. Success in thi=
s use case is defined by a reduction in the rate of re-entry into the homel=
ess system, which is quantitatively assessed through the predicted probabil=
ities generated by the BART model.</p><p>Implicit value claims within the u=
se case include the belief that algorithmic decision-making can lead to fai=
rer and more efficient outcomes than traditional methods. There is an under=
lying assumption that the data used for modeling is sufficiently comprehens=
ive and representative of the diverse needs of the homeless population. Add=
itionally, the use case assumes that the optimization process will not disp=
roportionately disadvantage any particular group of households, although th=
is assumption is challenged by the findings that indicate some households m=
ay experience increased probabilities of re-entry due to reallocation.</p><=
p>The use case also highlights the importance of fairness considerations, a=
cknowledging that while efficiency gains are achievable, they come with tra=
de-offs that may harm certain households. This dual focus on efficiency and=
 equity raises ethical questions about the implications of algorithmic deci=
sion-making in social service contexts, particularly in terms of how decisi=
ons are made and the potential for systemic biases to persist or worsen.</p=
><h2>2) The Framework, Reconstructed</h2><p>Contractualism, as articulated =
by T. M. Scanlon, posits that moral principles are those that no one could =
reasonably reject as a basis for informed, unforced, general agreement. Thi=
s framework emphasizes the importance of mutual recognition and respect for=
 individuals as rational agents capable of reasoning about justifications f=
or actions. The core principles of contractualism include the authority of =
moral standards derived from the capacity for rational agency, the focus on=
 wrongness as unjustifiability, and the rejection of aggregation in favor o=
f individual perspectives.</p><p>The scope of contractualism is primarily c=
oncerned with interpersonal relationships and the obligations that arise fr=
om them. It seeks to establish principles that govern how individuals ought=
 to treat one another, emphasizing the importance of justifying actions to =
others. The framework prioritizes the moral status of individuals based on =
their capacity for rational agency, which serves as the foundation for asse=
ssing the justifiability of actions and principles.</p><p>One of the non-ne=
gotiables of contractualism is the idea that moral requirements must be jus=
tifiable to each person affected by them. This principle underscores the im=
portance of considering the perspectives and reasons of individuals when ev=
aluating moral actions. Contractualism also rejects utilitarian aggregation=
, which often overlooks the unique moral claims of individuals in favor of =
